# The one Docker file not in docker/, and it cannot be. The build context is the
# repository root, because the build needs the source, and the root of the
# context is the only place every builder looks. BuildKit would also take
# docker/Dockerfile.dockerignore, but the legacy builder - what `docker build`
# still is wherever the buildx plugin is not installed - reads nothing else, and
# would then ship the whole tree, docker/.env and its TUNNEL_TOKEN included.

# Build output, everywhere. Without this the context is ~8 GB instead of ~200 MB.
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Note what is NOT here: .git. MinVer derives the version from the tags during
# `dotnet publish`, so excluding the history would make every image claim
# 0.1.0-alpha.0 - and do it silently. At ~40 MB it is the cheapest way to have
# `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` produce a correctly versioned image with
# no other flags.
# The Dockerfile's VERSION arg covers the shallow-clone case where it can't.

# Heads the server image has no use for. The publish only ever walks
# Flower.Server -> Flower.Core, plus Flower.Web -> Flower -> Flower.Core when
# the browser UI is included.
Flower.Desktop/
Flower.Android/
Flower.iOS/
Tests/

# Vendored miniaudio sources and their compiled .so/.framework outputs, for the
# mobile heads' audio sink. Nothing the server links against.
native/

docs/
.github/
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/
*.DotSettings.user
.DS_Store

# This directory: the Dockerfile, the compose files, the Caddyfile they mount -
# and, when the compose files are run from here, a running deployment's state:
# the .env holding TUNNEL_TOKEN, and caddy-data with Caddy's certificates and
# ACME account keys. Nothing the build reads, and the last thing that should be
# shipped into a build context.
docker/

# The server's DataDirectory, if it was run straight from a checkout - it holds
# the device key.
data/
